Exactly. Some things only count because they were noticed without being requested.

If you have to say, “See me,” “choose me,” “appreciate this,” or “you should know why this matters,” you may get the behavior afterward—but you’ve lost the evidence you were actually looking for: would they have perceived it and acted on it themselves?

That’s the difference between compliance and recognition.

The catch is that nobody has perfect access to another person’s internal world. So there’s a tension:

Unprompted action is stronger evidence. Communication is a better coordination mechanism.

Sometimes saying it does defeat the test. It doesn’t necessarily defeat the relationship.